import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Retro — the differential rewrite check (catopt's engine vs handl's)","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/retros/handl-differential-rewrite-check.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/retros/handl-differential-rewrite-check.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/retros/handl-differential-rewrite-check.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="retro-—-the-differential-rewrite-check-catopt-s-engine-vs-handl-s" tabindex="-1">Retro — the differential rewrite check (catopt&#39;s engine vs handl&#39;s) <a class="header-anchor" href="#retro-—-the-differential-rewrite-check-catopt-s-engine-vs-handl-s" aria-label="Permalink to “Retro — the differential rewrite check (catopt&#39;s engine vs handl&#39;s)”">​</a></h1><p><strong>Date</strong>: 2026-10-15. <strong>Assets</strong>: <code>lab/audit/handl_differential.py</code>, <code>tests/test_handl_peer.py</code>. <strong>Enabled by</strong>: the peer (plan 0030 step 2), which is what lets catopt drive a <em>second</em> rewrite engine at all.</p><h2 id="what-it-does" tabindex="-1">What it does <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-it-does" aria-label="Permalink to “What it does”">​</a></h2><p>For every law in the shipped bank it synthesizes one term matching the left-hand side, rewrites it with <strong>catopt&#39;s</strong> engine and with <strong>handl&#39;s</strong> (through the peer CLI), renders both results as <code>{kind, children}</code>, and compares them.</p><p>Two independent engines applying the same law to the same term is evidence one engine cannot give — the cheapest form of &quot;certified rewrite&quot; the project has. It is also the first thing that uses the integration for something other than proving the integration works.</p><h2 id="result-—-whole-bank-all-83-laws-accounted-for" tabindex="-1">Result — whole bank, all 83 laws accounted for <a class="header-anchor" href="#result-—-whole-bank-all-83-laws-accounted-for" aria-label="Permalink to “Result — whole bank, all 83 laws accounted for”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>bucket</th><th>count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>compared</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td><strong>agree</strong></td><td><strong>19</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>DISAGREE</strong></td><td><strong>0</strong></td></tr><tr><td>skipped (leaf rhs)</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>root-eliminating</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>declined by an engine</td><td>37</td></tr><tr><td>guard raised (synthesized term)</td><td>19</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Where both engines fire, they agree.</strong> The law bank survives an independent implementation — that is the headline, and it is a stronger statement than any single-engine test can make.</p><p>The 64 laws that could not be compared are each reported <strong>with a reason</strong>, and two of those reasons are findings.</p><h2 id="finding-1-—-root-eliminating-laws-cannot-be-expressed-5-laws" tabindex="-1">Finding 1 — root-eliminating laws cannot be expressed (5 laws) <a class="header-anchor" href="#finding-1-—-root-eliminating-laws-cannot-be-expressed-5-laws" aria-label="Permalink to “Finding 1 — root-eliminating laws cannot be expressed (5 laws)”">​</a></h2><p><code>double_neg</code> is <code>neg(neg(a)) → a</code>: the law <strong>drops its root</strong> and returns a subterm. handl&#39;s <code>TransformRule::Declarative</code> always <em>creates</em> a node — its <code>target_kind</code> is a <code>String</code> — so such a law has no faithful rule form. catopt used to emit <code>target_kind: None</code>, and the engines diverge.</p><p>Two ways out, and they are not equivalent:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>option</th><th>consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a rule form in handl (e.g. <code>Replace { path }</code>)</td><td>faithful; a handl-side design decision</td></tr><tr><td>a declared <code>catopt:alias</code> kind whose payload carries the path</td><td>loadable and lossless <em>via the <code>catopt:</code> record</em>, but handl&#39;s engine then sees an opaque created node — i.e. it <strong>hides</strong> the semantic difference</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The probe reports these laws <strong>separately</strong> rather than choosing for us. Papering over a divergence inside a tool whose entire purpose is to find divergences would defeat it.</p><h3 id="addendum-2026-10-16-—-what-target-kind-none-actually-did-and-the-fix" tabindex="-1">Addendum (2026-10-16) — what <code>target_kind: None</code> actually did, and the fix <a class="header-anchor" href="#addendum-2026-10-16-—-what-target-kind-none-actually-did-and-the-fix" aria-label="Permalink to “Addendum (2026-10-16) — what target_kind: None actually did, and the fix”">​</a></h3><p>The gap was investigated. Two results.</p><p><strong>1. <code>target_kind: None</code> was silently wrong — a bug in catopt&#39;s bridge.</strong><code>TransformRule::Declarative.target_kind</code> is a Rust <code>String</code> (not an <code>Option</code>), but <code>language_from_dir</code> parses <code>transforms.yaml</code> with <code>serde_yaml::from_str::&lt;Vec&lt;TransformDef&gt;&gt;</code>, and serde_yaml coerces the YAML <code>null</code> scalar into the <strong>string <code>&quot;null&quot;</code></strong>. So the row loaded, and handl&#39;s engine (<code>walk.rs::fire_untraced</code> → <code>step.rs::build_rewrite</code>, <code>kind: target_kind.to_string()</code>) <strong>fabricated a node of kind <code>&quot;null&quot;</code></strong>. Firing the old <code>double_neg</code> row on <code>neg(neg(a))</code> gave handl <code>{&#39;kind&#39;: &#39;null&#39;, &#39;children&#39;: [a]}</code> where catopt gives <code>a</code> — a <em>silent lie</em>, not a refusal (and handl&#39;s <code>validate_transforms</code> only <em>Warns</em> about the unknown kind, so assembly still succeeds).</p><p><strong>Fixed</strong> (catopt <code>8548243</code>): the bridge now <strong>refuses explicitly</strong> — for a bare-metavar rhs it emits <code>rule: !Native &quot;catopt:eliminate_root&quot;</code> naming the missing operation. handl&#39;s engine then rejects it loudly (<code>&quot;requires native rewrite &#39;catopt:eliminate_root&#39; but no native registry is wired&quot;</code>), the bank still loads (83 transforms), and the law still round-trips through <code>catopt.law</code>. The probe is unchanged — it classifies root-eliminating laws before firing — so the divergence stays visible, and is now <em>louder</em> rather than hidden.</p><p><strong>2. No faithful form exists in handl&#39;s <em>existing</em> vocabulary.</strong> Checked against the engine: <code>Splice</code> lifts a child&#39;s children but the <code>Declarative</code> node still exists; <code>KeepPath</code> is a <em>child step</em>, never the rewritten node; <code>Declarative</code> always creates, so a node cannot be replaced <em>by</em> a child. <code>TransformRule::Native</code> is the only mechanism that <em>can</em> return a subterm (<code>ctx.remap_child(i)</code> returns an existing <code>AstRef</code>), but the peer wires no registry (so it errors) and the implementation would be catopt&#39;s Rust — handl&#39;s engine would be running catopt&#39;s code, making the comparison <strong>circular</strong> for exactly these 5 laws. So <code>Native</code> is a <em>refusal marker</em>, not a fix; the honest fix is the handl-side <code>TransformRule::Replace { path }</code> proposal recorded in plan 0030 step 3a (a handl design decision — handl&#39;s <code>transform_def.rs</code> was deliberately not edited).</p><p>Differential numbers are <strong>unchanged</strong> by all of this: 19 agree / 0 disagree / 5 root-eliminating / 3 leaf-rhs skipped / 37 declined / 19 guard-raised.</p><p><strong>Related, unfixed (no bank law hits it):</strong> the bridge emits <code>node_kind: None</code> for a <em>bare-metavar lhs</em> (<code>TransformDef.node_kind</code> is also a <code>String</code>), which serde_yaml likewise coerces to the string <code>&quot;null&quot;</code>. No shipped law has a bare-metavar lhs (checked: 0), so it is latent; it is pinned only by the synthetic <code>test_bare_metavar_root_refuses_as_native</code>. A <code>node_kind</code> has no <code>!Native</code> analogue (it is the dispatch key), so this one has no clean refusal either — noted here rather than fixed.</p><h3 id="addendum-2-2026-10-16-—-the-rule-form-landed-the-5-laws-are-compared" tabindex="-1">Addendum 2 (2026-10-16) — the rule form landed; the 5 laws are compared <a class="header-anchor" href="#addendum-2-2026-10-16-—-the-rule-form-landed-the-5-laws-are-compared" aria-label="Permalink to “Addendum 2 (2026-10-16) — the rule form landed; the 5 laws are compared”">​</a></h3><p>The <code>Replace { path }</code> proposal was taken by handl: <strong>handl ADR-0127</strong> (<code>crates/handl-language/src/fragments/transform_def.rs</code> for the variant, one arm in <code>plugin-engine-transform-rewrite/src/walk.rs::fire_untraced</code>, validation in <code>plugin-engine-transform/src/validate.rs</code>). An unresolvable path is a hard error, never a fabricated node — the failure mode Finding 1 records is now impossible by construction.</p><p>catopt&#39;s bridge emits <code>!Replace { path }</code> for a bare-metavar rhs bound in the lhs (the metavar&#39;s lhs path), so the whole bank maps faithfully; the <code>!Native</code> refusal survives only for a bare-metavar rhs with no bound path (no shipped law).</p><p><strong>The probe changed in two honest ways</strong> (it hides nothing — <code>DISAGREE</code> stays 0):</p><ol><li>the obsolete <code>_eliminates_root</code> pre-classification is gone; the laws are fired and compared like any other structural law;</li><li>the &quot;structural laws only&quot; filter now filters the <strong>rhs</strong> only — its documented intent. Filtering the lhs too had needlessly skipped four laws whose <em>lhs</em> carries a leaf but whose rhs is structural (<code>id_add</code>, <code>id_mul</code>, <code>softsign_fold</code>, <code>pow_to_square</code>); a leaf in the synthesized <strong>input</strong> is perfectly comparable.</li></ol><p><strong>Re-measured — whole bank, all 83 laws accounted for:</strong></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>bucket</th><th>before</th><th>after</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>compared</td><td>19</td><td><strong>24</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>agree</strong></td><td><strong>19</strong></td><td><strong>24</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>DISAGREE</strong></td><td><strong>0</strong></td><td><strong>0</strong></td></tr><tr><td>skipped (leaf rhs)</td><td>3</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>declined by an engine</td><td>37</td><td>39</td></tr><tr><td>guard raised (synthesized term)</td><td>19</td><td>19</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>root-eliminating</code> is no longer a bucket (nothing is skipped for it); the probe now prints the five per law:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>  root-eliminating laws (bare-metavar rhs — handl !Replace, ADR-0127):</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    - id_add: agree</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    - id_mul: agree</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    - double_neg: agree</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    - transpose_noop: declined</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    - chunk_single: declined</span></span></code></pre></div><p>So <strong>three of the five agree inside the probe</strong>; <code>transpose_noop</code> and <code>chunk_single</code> are <strong>declined by catopt&#39;s own engine</strong> on the probe&#39;s synthesized term — their guards (<code>axes-noop</code>, <code>attr-eq CK 1</code>) need concrete attrs the generic synthesis cannot supply (that is Finding 2, not a divergence). Given a guard-satisfying term <strong>both engines return the subterm</strong>, so <strong>all five agree wherever both fire</strong> — pinned by <code>tests/test_handl_peer.py::test_root_eliminating_laws_agree_on_satisfying_terms</code>.</p><p>The 19→24 gain is the five root-eliminating laws (minus the two the guards decline) plus the four the lhs-filter had hidden.</p><h2 id="finding-2-—-one-synthesized-term-per-law-is-not-universally-sound" tabindex="-1">Finding 2 — one synthesized term per law is not universally sound <a class="header-anchor" href="#finding-2-—-one-synthesized-term-per-law-is-not-universally-sound" aria-label="Permalink to “Finding 2 — one synthesized term per law is not universally sound”">​</a></h2><p>19 laws&#39; guards <strong>raise</strong> on a synthesized term (bare <code>f64</code> variables): they are <em>shape-sensitive</em> — they consult the shape of a child, and <code>term.typ.shape</code> on a bare variable is not there. Counted separately so it cannot mask a real disagreement.</p><p>That is a limitation of the <em>probe</em>, not of either engine, and it points at the obvious improvement: synthesize terms the guards accept (or run the comparison on real corpus terms, where shapes are known).</p><h2 id="what-this-does-not-prove" tabindex="-1">What this does <strong>not</strong> prove <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-this-does-not-prove" aria-label="Permalink to “What this does not prove”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Exhaustive equivalence.</strong> One term per law is a smoke-level check; it looks for disagreement, and one would be enough to matter.</li><li><strong>That the laws are <em>correct</em>.</strong> This compares two engines&#39; <em>rewriting</em>, not their semantics — the numeric oracle and the certificate replay are what say a law is true. A law can be faithfully rewritten by both engines and still be wrong.</li><li><strong>Anything about execution.</strong> No tensors cross (ADR 0011).</li></ul><h2 id="value" tabindex="-1">Value <a class="header-anchor" href="#value" aria-label="Permalink to “Value”">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>A new asset</strong>: cross-engine agreement over the bank, measured, with the non-comparable laws named.</li><li><strong>A regression guard</strong>: <code>tests/test_handl_peer.py</code> pins <strong>24 agree / 0 disagree</strong> (was 19 — Addendum 2), so a change in <em>either</em> engine that breaks agreement fails a test.</li><li><strong>One gap closed, one named</strong>: the root-eliminating rule form was a real representational gap in handl&#39;s vocabulary and is now <strong>closed</strong> (handl ADR-0127); the shape-sensitive guard limitation (a probe improvement) remains and is what still keeps <code>transpose_noop</code>/<code>chunk_single</code> out of the compared set.</li></ol>`,39)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};